                    NOTES FOR GCC2 USAGE UNDER OS-9/68k                    ===================================The files---------    GNU/CMDS/cc2               C compiler    GNU/CMDS/cc2plus           C++ compiler    GNU/CMDS/cccp2             preprocessor for C & C++    GNU/CMDS/gcc2              compiler executive    GNU/CMDS/collect           global con-/de-structor collector    LIB/libgcc.l               GCC support library (for use with old clibn.l)    LIB/gclibXXX.l             mix of new IO library, libgcc and old clib.l    LIB/libgppXXX.l            C++ class library    LIB/libiostreamXXX.l       C++ stream library    DEFS/GCC2/*.h              varargs for use with gcc & gcc2    DEFS/GPP2/*                C++ include filesThe cccp2 preprocessors's search order for include files--------------------------------------------------------     <-I from commandline>     <C_INCLUDE_PATH environment> or <GCCDEF environment>      (C   only)     <CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH environment> or <GPPDEF environment>  (C++ only)     /DD/DEFS/GPP2 (gpp2 only)     /DD/DEFS/GCC2     /DD/DEFS   The environment variable GCCDEF is only used, if C_INCLUDE_PATH is not set.   If several directories are specified in these environment variables, they   must be separated by colons.The gcc2 compiler's search rules for libraries----------------------------------------------   If a library is specified using the '-l<name>' syntax, gcc2 will search   for the following files in the following order:     <dir>/<name>     <dir>/<name>.l     (only searched if <name> does not contain a dot '.')     <dir>/lib<name>.l  (only searched if <name> does not contain a dot '.')   <dir> is one of the following names (order of usage) :     -L<dir>            (in the same order as they appear in the command line)     <GCCLIB environment>     /dd/LIB     /h0/LIB     /dd/GNULIB     /h0/GNULIB   If <name> is not found using the above rules, gcc2 passes -l=<name> to the   linker; otherwise, the complete path name of the library file is composed   and passed to the linker.Temporary files---------------   The gcc2 compiler uses the directory specified in the environment variable   TMPDIR for temporary files. This setting can be overridden by using the   -T<tmpdir> command line option. If neither is set, temporary files are   created in the current working directory.Trouble shooting----------------    Q : The cc2 compiler terminates with stack overflow.    A : Specify -Fcc2s200 (-Fcc2pluss200) or similar in order to increase        the compiler's stack size.    Q : The option -mlong-calls does not work for a 68000 target.    A : This option requires at least version 2.9 of the r68 assembler        (this applies also to the -ucc and -ob options).    Q : A program compiled with gcc2 terminates with Error #000:106 !    A : This is a stack overflow condition, if the program was compiled with        the -m68020 option. The gcc2 compiler uses an optimized technique        for stack checking (chk2 instruction). The -mstdstack-check option        can be specified to switch to the older (slower) stack checking code.        Some older versions of libgcc.l showed a similar problem, if the main        function used a lot of local variables.    Q : After installation of a new version of G++, old programs cannot be        linked anymore.    A : Internal symbol names may change from one version to another        (virtual function tables or mangled function names). New G++ versions        require, therefore, that an unconditional make (-u) is done at least        once.    Q : The linker complains about unresolved references when trying to link        C++ programs (-++lib).    A : Due to problems in l68 (old version) and l68/libgen (new versions),        some libraries have to be specified twice (libgppXXX.l) or even three        times (libiostreamXXX.l).    Q : Can I use the new C-libraries from UltraC?    A : YES, but only the standard libraries, the current version of libgen        (Ed #7) cannot handle common declarations. The collect programs        gives a warning that it cannot yet handle this type of library.    Q : A programm compiled with gcc2 only works properly when compiled        without optimization.    A : Some library functions in clibXXXX.l do not save all registers they        use (for example strtod). Please use newer version of the library.    Q : A program that contains inline assembly code can only be compiled with        or without the -ucc option, because of the required register prefix.    A : There is a macro in the preprocessor that returns the register prefix.        Write for example :          __asm("move.l "__REGISTER_PREFIX__"d0,-("__REGISTER_PREFIX__"a7)");        or better          #define R __REGISTER_PREFIX__          __asm("move.l "R"d0,-("R"a7)");        which is much shorter if you have more than one __asm statement.    Q : Can .r files compiled with old stdio.h be linked with new .r files ?    A : YES, the new I/O system contains code for compatibility with the old        stdio.Special OSK options-------------------  -mremote           generate long data references  -mlong-calls       generate long code references  -mnostack-check    omit stack checking code  -mstdstack-check   use standard stack checking code for 68020  -mnocom            do not emit declarations of common data  -mgss              generate assembler labels for static functions  -++lib             link to the standard C++ libraries  -oldlibs           use the orignal clib instead of the new gclibXXX.l.                     Be caseful not to include GCC2/stdio.h in your programs,                     or I/O problems may arise (-I/dd/defs solves the problem)                     Do NOT use with -++lib.  -col               the collector output file is not deleted  -gsrcdbg           generate .dbg file for srcdbg  -gg                generate .dbg file for default debugger (currently srcdbg)  -g                 generate .stb module for debug.  -F<prog>s<stack>p<prior>o<opts>                     call <prog> with additional <stack>, priority <prior> and                     options <opts>                     -Fl68s100p200o-sm will result in 'l68 #100 ^200 -sm'-------------------------------------------------------------------------------         NOTES FOR GCC V2.x.x compilation under OS-9/68k         =============================================== - the script 'make_all' creates all necessary directories and then compiles   all three stages of gcc V2.x. For the first stage you need gcc V1.42   (cc1 Ed #30 needed to avoid 'illegal % message').   For a 'all stages' compilation of gcc2 you need the following sources   installed in your system :     /dd/GNU/GCC_2.x.x     /dd/GNU/GCLIB     /dd/GNU/LIBGPP_2.x.x - use gcc Ed #12 or remove '-A /dd/lib/cstart.r' from the makefile. - you also need a UNIX compatibility library like os9lib (including its   header files); the directory where these include files are expected   is specified as -I/dd/defs/os9lib in the makefile (please change if   necessary). - if you want to use 'make' directly you have to set the macro 'srcdir' in   the makefile. - if your computer runs out of memory, omit the -ob option from the OPT   macro [makefile]. - '-fbytecode' always results in a buserror. turn it off in tm.h until I   know the underlying problem of this error (not yet documented). - cc2plus seems to work fine, cc2obj has not yet been tested (93/12/06)PLEASE SEND ALL CHANGES MADE TO THE GCC SOURCES TO ME TO ENSURE THAT THEYARE INCLUDED IN THE NEXT RELEASE. E-mail to 'stp@bernina.ethz.ch' or write toStephan Paschedag, Webermuehle 43/334, CH-5432 Neuenhof, Switzerland.